identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22045582
    description: "v0.2.5 — version DOI"
abstract: >-
  Exact biosequence dynamic programming is thought too slow to run at scale, but the cost was never
  the arithmetic — it is the dependency structure of the natural traversal. Filling a matrix row by
  row makes every cell wait on its neighbour, an order no hardware can widen. Sweeping anti-diagonals
  instead leaves a whole front independent; folding sweeps by span and cofolding by window length,
  collapsing a sextic recurrence into a few hundred dependent layers with full parallelism inside
  each. AffineGaps carries that reordering through to the GPU, so the exact optimum arrives at speeds
  usually reserved for heuristics.

  It covers Needleman-Wunsch and Smith-Waterman under Gotoh's affine gap model, Levenshtein edit
  distance, Zuker minimum free energy folding over the Turner tables, and Sankoff's simultaneous
  alignment and folding of two RNA sequences, each reconstructing its traceback rather than a score
  alone. Alignment does so in linear memory; Sankoff provably cannot, which bounds it to short
  sequences and makes it the oracle the banded approximations are measured against.
